WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.010
At the peak of the last glacial, glaciers
covered about 32 per cent of Earth's

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:05.850
land.

3
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.010
Today, about 10 per cent.

4
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:13.770
In England, ice sheets dumped the boulder
clay that now forms the Holderness cliffs.

5
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:20.010
Over the last 800,000 years, warm spells
like today's came roughly every 100,000

6
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.070
years.

7
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.930
Nobody was there to measure it.

8
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.530
So how do scientists know?

9
00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:26.610
Dinosaur fossils?

10
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.550
Guesswork?

11
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.210
In this mission, you'll go to Antarctica,

12
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.710
where drills pull up ice that
reaches back 800,000 years.

13
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.250
Then you'll read a core layer by layer,
and judge how far to trust what it tells

14
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.110
you.

15
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.640
Your mission: find out how scientists read
Earth's climate from long before thermometers.
